  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1150 AM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nOne more day (for now) of hot weather. High temperatures this\nafternoon will continue to run at least 10 degrees above average.\nBut the upper level ridge that has brought us the hot weather is\ndrifting off to our west this afternoon and that will allow a\nlittle moisture to work in from the north to give us a little\nbetter rain chances this afternoon and evening. Then on Saturday\nthat is when things start to get interesting.\n\nSeveral factors are coming together to increase the risk for\nstrong thunderstorms and heavy rain on Saturday. First the upper \nlevel ridge will be centered over northern Arizona. This will give\nus a north flow aloft. Anytime you get north (or south) flow it \nraises the concerns for training (one storm after another) storms\nespecially in the mountains. At the surface we will have a \nwashed out cold front just to our east which could help with some\nstorm development, but the better storm maker, will be a short \nwave trough, that will drop south out of Colorado Saturday \nevening. Precipitable water values (PW's) will be in the 1.2 to \n1.4 inches range, which is good but not great for flash flooding. \nAgain though I think we will see multiple storms develop in the \nmountains and then drop south. One storm will pop-up and drop 0.50\nof rain and then move south, followed by another storm, followed \nby another storm. The Flood Watch includes all of Otero and El \nPaso Counties and the western portion of Hudspeth County as storms\nform in the Sacs they will drop south into the lowlands. There is\nsome indication that we may need to extended the Flood Watch into\nDona Ana County as the mountains to the north of and east of Las \nCruces may help enhance some of rainfall in that county. In \naddition to the heavy rain threat, we will see the potential for a\nfew strong to sever thunderstorms. They main threats will be \nlarge hail and strong outflow winds. \n\nFor Sunday, the upper level ridge will be in about the same place,\nbut we will have drier and more stable air move into the region\nso the rain threat will be lower. For Monday and Tuesday, the \nupper level ridge will be centered over central Arizona. This \nelongated ridge will allow some moisture to be pulled in from the \neast so we will see a little better chances for rain for the start\nof next week. The upper level ridge won't move much for next \nweek, so we will continue to see a slight chance for afternoon \nevening thunderstorms, mainly confined in area mountains. \n\nLooking at our high temperatures, today looks to be the last for\nour really hot weather. For Saturday and Sunday, we will see high\ntemperatures still running a few degrees above average, which may\nallow a few locations to hit 100, but most locations will stay in\nthe mid to upper 90's. With the upper level dropping a little \nfurther south into central Arizona for the start of next week, \nthat will allow our high temperatures to creep a few degrees \nhigher. So triple digit highs are back in the forecast for much of\nthe lowlands for next week, though it does not look to be as hot \nas this week has been.",
